E. Jay O'Connell's doubts about the value of political speech brought
up something that haunts me--I defend libertarianism because it makes
the most sense to me, but I keep wondering if I'm right....Marxism
was such a disaster, and maybe libertarianism is another radical
approach that's leaving something important out, though I admit
that the point at which I have doubts is the break between
anarchism and minarchism. 

I'm an anarchist, but I keep thinking about how the Yugoslavians were
better off with a tight central government. I don't think that
anarchism can work unless there is some way of stopping people
who want to form governments before they get very far--L. Neil
Smith seemed to think in one of his sf novels that it's legitimate
to kill anyone who makes a serious attempt to start a government--
he may be right.

To focus this doubt a little more firmly, *how* do you judge ideas
about how human groups should be structured? The stakes are high
and controlled experiment is impossible.

Nancy Lebovitz

