Date: Sun, 5 Dec 93 13:57:33 EST
From: eli@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: The Decline and Fall of the Extropians List

Sheesh, what a bunch of sourpusses!

I certainly have not found every discussion here to be of the utmost
importance to my life, by I have no intention of dropping the list
anytime soon. (I'm sure you're all relieved to hear that. :-)

Though I can't follow along as easily when people discuss AIT, and
though I have no personal interest in extropian shoes, proper bath
tubbing (and hot tubbing), or whether the list justice gripe of
the week is well- or poorly-founded, I still find the list as a
whole quite valuable.

First of all, the list provides access to people with lots of
great experience in many different areas. You can't buy that
kind of networking.

Second, I like being able to discuss things with a group of people
who are not mentally trussed by today's political correctness,
social or intellectual convention, or simple narrow-mindedness. Let's
not forget how rare a commodity that is. (Try asking your dentist
seriously about how many decades you'll get out of a new dental
appliance. He'll give you a very puzzled look if you complain
that two or three decades doesn't seem like a very long time.)

Finally, I attribute at least three major changes in my life to
exposure to this list. In the year or so that I've been here, I've
moved more from talking about things to doing them, and I'm very happy
about it. My three big self-transformations for this year are:

	Health: I'm attempting (with some success) to turn a 200 lb.
	weakling into a 175lb. muscle-man. (Well, "muscle-man" may
	be overstating it, but I am quite a bit stronger and leaner.)

	Rights: I've finally gotten serious about exercising my
	rights as a free (hah) citizen to own and operate firearms.
	I'm hoping to buy myself a handgun sometime before the Brady
	Law goes fully into effect. (I may even convince my wife
	to try it out, too.)

	Learning: I'm also trying to turn this poor hacker into a
	proper businessman. Capitalism as a political perspective
	is nice, but not as nice as Capitalism as a team sport.
	(This is by far the hardest of the three. Reading _Crossing
	the Chasm_ today, I learned why I never seem to be in sync
	with the majority of the market.)

In summary, I think that the particular messages appearing on the list
from day to day are only part of the value of the list, and I intend
to stick around.

Elias Israel
