Date: Sun, 05 Dec 93 14:30:30 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: Love and Touch: Opening the channel

Martha Graham around 1945: "There is a vitality, an energy, a quickening 
that is translated through you into action, and because there is only 
one of you in all of time, this expression is unique. If you block it, 
it will never exist through any other medium and it will be lost. The 
world will not have it. It is not your business to determine how good it 
is nor how valuable nor how it compares with other expressions. It is 
your business to keep it yours clearly and directly, to keep the channel 
open. You do not even have to believe in yourself or your work. You have 
to keep yourself open and aware to the urges that motivate you. Keep the 
channel open."

As someone who made great expressive use of her body, MGraham might not 
have been attracted to e-forums in their current state. Diffuse lessons 
can be drawn from this: 1) we should all be leaping and stretching and 
expanding the range of our own spectrum of expressiveness. or 
2) pay close attention to whether participating in list-reading is in 
fact opening you up to your own individual voice. consider experimental 
evidence on the difference between enervation and vigor in the 
presentation of experience. 

strangely, reading smart people post very clever comments on nearly 
apposite topics may not be any more envigorating than listening to very 
great musicians perform on CDs and in concert halls, or frequenting 
lectures without forging a personal understanding.

it seems far more important to produce, at whatever level of capacity, 
than to consume. i will just assert this as a bald value commitment of 
mine, although one can see this decision activated in economies, 
ecologies, evolutionary histories, productive attacks on intellectual 
problems which had to begin with some clearly limited and obviously 
wrong formulation....

i generalize the eye/touch to the body/mind relation: the mind is the 
omni-informavore, capable of endlessly delaying action until it has 
gathered all relevant material. bc of the way language and thinking 
work, everything is potnetially relevant. left to its internal logic, 
the mind proposes to delay and devour all that has already been 
generated. the motivation to create novelty comes from the body's 
impatience and urgency to produce against the cut of experienced 
limitations.

it amuses me that the mind, when pressed for output, can spin out the 
"solution" of first figuring out how to live forever, before getting 
started on the project of how to live.

this isn't a cut against others, since everyone on the list has at least 
some sort of relationship with their body, and that guarantees that they 
are creating as well as consuming. but the world of e-text, with its 
capacity for locating very narrow niches of interest, risks dampening 
all but a few people's motivation to produce thoughts. 

true, this mono-culturization began with writing-- yesterday i listened  
to an african folklorist, Dr. Mbele. his fluid vibrancy and deep 
enjoyment of talking, telling stories, taking time to spin them again, 
underlines my perceived distinction between the text and the breath. 

i am not advocating a Rousseavian return to a world without scientific 
american or the internet. no matter how actualized one is, there is a 
role for input from others. but the way this gets mediated seems to 
imply what it means to construct a community, how one goes about 
creating consensual interaction that amplifies and feeds into the 
members' separate goals. good cults and bad cults, maybe

