Date: Sun, 5 Dec 93 12:03:52 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Decline and Fall

Some responses to my post about the apparent decline of the quality of
debate have missed my point.

- I was not arguing for "posting minimums," that everyone would have
to post some minimum quota of posts...a crazy idea..

- My question to the "250 or so lurkers" was intended to literally ask
them what it is they get from the list, as the debate is apparently
not triggering them to add to the debate. (For the record, Harry tells
us there are about 330 people on the list. There seem to be about 30
very frequent posters, roughly, and another 50 who post a comment or
even a mini-essay every few weeks or so. But that leaves a few
hundred subscribers who essentially never say anything. I can't
imagine being in such a situation, naturally, so I am curious about
what keeps lurkers on the list.)

So, the folks who have implied that I am arguing for a posting quota,
or am criticizing the lurkers, are off base.

I agree with Perry's wonderful analogy that the List is a bit like a
party that was glorious fun at the beginning but is now dragging, with
people getting tired and irritable.

The solution may be for some of us to radically deescalate our
postings. Perry is doing this, the folks who've left have already done
this of course, and maybe this is normal.

But what mostly concerns me is that the outflow of "idea people"
(Finney, Morrow, Ringuette, Hanson, half a dozen others) has only
_partly_ been compensated for by the inflow of new idea people, people
like Doug Barnes, Kennita Watson (though she calls herself the
"Anti-Tim"!), and others. Maybe this means it's time to _solicit_ new
members, new core contributors.

Presumably this is one effect desired from the growing circulation of
"Extropy" magazine, but I'm not so sure we're seeing it. Frankly, I
see no concrete evidence that "Extropy" is playing much of a role in
bringing new active posters to the List. Are there folks out there who
came to the mailing list by reading "Extropy"? Speak up!

If the circulation increase in "Extropy" is nice, and impressive, but
is not the best way to accumulate activist posters (and a circulation
of, say, 4000 may just not be an adequate subscription base to do
this), then maybe we need to find other ways to reinvigorate the list.

(I make the assumption, supported by Perry's comments, the comments of
others, and (perhaps) by the departure of key participants, that the
list is sorely in need of some new vigor, that we are not just seeing
a "seasonal lull." The seasonal lulls usually seem to happen at the
time of Xmas and whatnot, not a month or more earlier. In any case,
the deeper issue is not one of a seasonal slow-down in volume, but of
a general "exhaustion" of topics. (The new topic on touchy-feely,
tactile aspects of life is "unusual," but is probably a good thing in
that it at least explores a new topic...I hereby promise to keep my
mouth shut on this topic, though.)

So, all organizations and clubs face times when they need to
reinvigorate, recruit new blood, have membership drives, kindle new
debates, and so on.

I think this time has come for Extropians.

I, like Elias Israel, have gotten a lot out of the list in the year
and a half I've been on it (more or less). I especially have enjoyed
meeting--in person (how UnExtropian!)--many fine people. The Extropian
parties, luncheons, outings, etc., have been welcome. And meeting East
Coasters like Perry Metzger, Harry Shapiro, Sasha Chislenko, Simon!
Levy, and many others has been a great joy to me.

Sadly, though, debate on this list has gotten pretty boring for me. I
still comment, but perhaps I'm just going through the motions, on
auto-pilot. Like Perry, maybe it's time for me to just throttle way
back on posting and do more of other things.

I just got an Apple Newton yesterday, my new Symantec C++ package is
on its way (to test out Schneier's crypto code), the
Kolmogorov-Chaitin complexity book is superb, my "investment" and
"beating the market" work continues, just got Knuth's book on graph
theory ("GraphBase"), Cypherpunks is still hopping (despite the
Detweiler Perversion's invasion from the Unthinking Depths), and of
course there's a whole lot of other stuff to be done. (Like finishing
my novel, even if there's no market for it!)

(By the way, for the Bay Aryans out there, I'll be giving a lecture at
Stanford on crypto and crypto anarchy this coming Wednesday, December
8th, at 4:15 p.m., at Skilling Auditorium. Open to the public.)

Hard for me to believe I call myself "retired"!

Good luck, "'Tropes," and may you bend toward the light.


--Tim

