Date: Sun, 5 Dec 1993 20:27:37 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: work and slavery

Is there an extropically-friendly theory of work out there somewhere?

I've been thinking that one of the reasons that bright, individualist
people tend to be underemployed and in financially difficult situations
is a simple unwillingness to be enslaved.

Consider: my main argument with 'benign' government is that it allocates
my time for me and I don't like that (if I pay a third of my income out
in taxes then that actually means that a third of my workday is devoted
to goals over which I have no control); if half of my waking life is spent
at or preparing for 'work' then the third of that over which I have no
control makes me quite literally a 1/6 slave, and slavery is unsuitable
for a thinking being.

However, I can't help but push this same thing further and consider
what most people actually do during that half of their waking lives
that they are taking orders from somebody else and, in exchange for
money, actively pursue somebody else's agenda.  The simple examples
are people like my grandmother, who spent some unbelievable number
of years as a bottlecap inspector where, for eight hours a day or
more if overtime was available she directed her concentration
toward a stream of freshly-bent metal.  She considered herself
a fortunate and fulfilled person until she retired and died a
year later.

Now, as for me, there's no way that I'm going to do something like
that -- I can't even imagine a material good that could compensate
for such a waste of half my waking time.  In fact, even if the work
is "interesting" and even when I can tell myself that I'm gaining
skills that will be helpful to me in the future, I still find that
I'm kind of resistant to throwing chunks of my life at somebody
else's agenda.

Now, I do realize and fully support the usual response to this
kind of thinking -- people make a contract to spend their time
working for somebody else in exchange for something they want
and they judge how much of their time various things are worth.
But it doesn't surprise me too much that people who are particularly
upset by external control of any kind, who love "freedom", who
rarely if ever get bored when left to their own devices, and so
on, might balance a willingness to trade time for things rather
differently than somebody who's fulfilled by watching bottle
caps flash by because at least they're busy.

I have to admit that a decent computer, a decent compiler, a
Net connection, access to a great library, some good friends,
a decent place to live, and good health care, halfway decent
food, and some small but unspecified amount of spending money
are all that I really need to keep me "happy" -- so (until
recently at least) I've tended to balance things in such a way
that that's all I have, and I'd usually only need a 15-hour
"workweek" to achieve that (new car?  who the hell needs a
new car?)

I know a bus driver who recently bought a $30,000 boat so
sail around a local lake -- I'm thinking jeez, I'd rather
spend that on two years of no-time-commitment life!  What
a wonderful two years that would be! (for the record, he
likes the boat and will get some of that money back when
he sells it, but he bought it new and it'll depreciate
a great deal).

People make their own tradeoffs, of course -- I'm just saying
that there's a good argument for choosing a small income in
exchange for a small time commitment.

Of course, there's more to the story than just this.  Many
and hopefully most people find their slavery time to be
rewarding in nonmonetary ways.  Some people even go so far
as to claim that they're paid to do exactly what they'd
want to be doing anyway, but I don't believe it -- perhaps
there are some people fortunate enough to spend half of their
paid time on things they'd actually do if freed from all
commitments attached to their income (hard to imagine that
people would choose to write all those reports and memos,
stay to the end of every meeting, talk to every client, and
so on if they didn't "have" to).  The quick test for this
kind of thing is to look for slave phrases like "I have to
get this done" or "I need to do xxx for a while now" or
"I have to work tonight".  For those fortunates who can
spend half of their time on things they'd do on their own
anyway, we can think of them as having twice the hourly
wage they might seem to have.  Similarly, people who
are building skills or a "career" can simply roll the
future benefits into their current hourly wage to see
(only with hindsight, alas) the dollar value they place
on their time.

Of course, it's some consolation that if carefully spent
a half million dollars is probably plenty to retire on
as soon as it's been accumulated (speaking of which, I'm
very interested in figures like that -- suppose I was
content with the equivalent of $20,000 1993 dollars per
year extended forever; given that one is willing to
take some small risk in investment strategy, how much
capital would be required to get that return?)

So, is there a theory of work out there that deals with these
issues?  Has this topic been discussed here in the dim past?

derek
